WEBVTT

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:11.760
<v Announcer>A quick warning, there are curse words that are unbeeped in today's episode of the show. If you prefer a beeped version, you can find that at our website, thisamericanlife.org.
<v Announcer>小提示：今天节目中会有未经消音的脏话。如果您更喜欢消音版本，可以前往我们的网站 thisamericanlife.org 收听。

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:32.800
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. I recently heard this podcast that was so exciting to hear, because it felt like the people who did it invented something completely new, which is so rare. And it's like, I feel like they took what shows like ours do, and then just pushed it further and further until it became something else.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥 WBEZ 电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我最近听了一档播客，听的时候感到非常兴奋，因为感觉制作这档节目的人发明了一些全新的东西，这在当下非常罕见。感觉他们汲取了像我们这类节目的精髓，然后不断将其推向极致，直到它变成了某种全新的东西。

00:00:32.800 --> 00:01:07.210
<v Ira Glass>And I want to play you some of that show today. It's by somebody who I had never heard of before. His name is Sayre Quevedo. And the series is called Reaching Out. It's made by Futuro Studios. And as you'll hear, at some point years ago, it seems like Sayre started recording nearly all of his personal conversations, including these especially passive aggressive conversations with his twin brother that get more interesting and complicated the further you get into the series. And it's just-- I don't know-- real, messy life captured on recordings in a way that is rare to hear.
<v Ira Glass>今天我想播放一些该节目的片段。作者是一位我此前从未听说过的人，他叫塞尔·克韦多（Sayre Quevedo）。该系列名为《接触》（Reaching Out），由 Futuro 工作室制作。正如你接下来会听到的，几年前，塞尔似乎开始录制他几乎所有的私人对话，包括那些与他双胞胎兄弟之间特别带有被动攻击意味的对话，这些对话在你越深入了解该系列时就越显得复杂且有趣。那真的是——我不知道该怎么形容——在录音中捕捉到了真实、混乱的生活，这在当下非常罕见。

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:39.330
<v Ira Glass>And then Sayre also invented this way to do narration that at first, when you hear it, you're like, what's happening? And then-- I don't know-- I really came to love it. And the show is ambitious. I don't know if I've heard another podcast that tries to capture a person's life as completely as this one does, and just what it feels like to be alive right now. So we're going to excerpt some of the show today. That's going to be the whole hour. And I just want to say, stay with it. I guarantee, it pays off. Here's Sayre.
<v Ira Glass>此外，塞尔还发明了一种叙述方式，起初当你听到时，你会想：这到底是怎么回事？然后——我不知道——我真的开始喜欢上了它。这档节目雄心勃勃。我不知道是否还听过另一档播客，能像这档节目一样如此完整地捕捉一个人的生活，以及此刻活着到底是什么感觉。所以今天我们将摘录该节目的一些片段，这将占据整个一小时的节目。我想说的是，请坚持听下去，我保证这非常值得。下面是塞尔。

00:01:39.330 --> 00:02:02.610
<v Sayre Quevedo>On February 15, 2020. Connor calls you to ask if you want to look for your birth father. You do not record this conversation. This is notable, if only because so much of your life has been recorded, moments far less consequential than this-- you and Connor's fourth birthday--
<v Sayre Quevedo>2020年2月15日。康纳打电话给你，问你想不想寻找你的生父。你没有录下这段对话。这一点很值得注意，哪怕只是因为你生命中有太多时刻都被录了下来，那些远不如这一刻重要的时刻——你和康纳的四岁生日——

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:08.270
<v Clown>You earned a rubber nose. Can you guys say that? Then hand me a rubber nose.
<v Clown>你赢得了一个橡胶鼻子。你们能说一下吗？然后递给我一个橡胶鼻子。

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:12.550
<v Sayre Quevedo>A school performance-- you play a scared rabbit. Your only line--
<v Sayre Quevedo>一场学校演出——你扮演一只受惊的兔子。你唯一的台词——

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:13.930
<v Young Sayre>I think we're lost.
<v Young Sayre>我想我们迷路了。

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.190
<v Kid>Don't worry. We'll just go back the way we came.
<v Kid>别担心。我们原路返回就好了。

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:18.010
<v Young Sayre>Which way did we come?
<v Young Sayre>我们是从哪边来的？

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:22.650
<v Sayre Quevedo>A house party when you were 16, when you first began to record yourself.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你16岁时的一场家庭派对，那是你第一次开始录下自己的声音。

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:33.450
<v Teenage Sayre>So I interview people and I talk to them about whatever I want to talk about. And since it's about me and it's about the things I do, I figure I should record this.
<v Teenage Sayre>所以我采访人们，和他们谈论任何我想谈的话题。既然是关于我，是关于我所做的事情，我想我应该录下来。

00:02:33.450 --> 00:03:14.050
<v Sayre Quevedo>There is a price for what we choose to remember and what we choose to forget. If you had known how this would all turn out, maybe you would have recorded everything. Or if you had known the cost, maybe you would not have recorded anything at all. On February 15, 2020, your brother calls you to ask if you want to look for your birth father, but there is no record of that call. There is, however, a recording of the call you make a few days later.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我们选择记住什么和选择遗忘什么，都是有代价的。如果你早知道这一切会如何收场，也许你当时会录下一切。或者如果你早知道代价，也许你根本就不会录下任何东西。2020年2月15日，你兄弟打电话问你想不想找你的生父，但那次通话没有记录。不过，几天后你给他回的那个电话确实有录音。

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:27.200
<v Sayre Quevedo>[PHONE DIALING]
<v Sayre Quevedo>[电话拨号声]

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.100
<v Connor>Hello?
<v Connor>喂？

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.700
<v Sayre Quevedo>Hey, brother.
<v Sayre Quevedo>嘿，兄弟。

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:32.740
<v Connor>Hey, what's up?
<v Connor>嘿，怎么了？

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:36.140
<v Sayre Quevedo>Nothing. My phone is at 10%, by the way.
<v Sayre Quevedo>没什么。顺便说一下，我手机只剩10%的电了。

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:42.140
<v Connor>Oh, that's cool. I don't feel like talking on the phone very long. I'm tired.
<v Connor>哦，那挺好的。我也不想在电话里聊太久。我很累。

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:45.620
<v Sayre Quevedo>I'm recording. I hope that is OK.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我在录音。希望没问题。

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.260
<v Connor>Oh, really? OK. Hmm.
<v Connor>哦，真的吗？好吧。嗯。

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.700
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah.
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的。

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:55.380
<v Connor>All right, that's cool. Are you recording because you want to talk about dad stuff?
<v Connor>好吧，那挺酷的。你录音是因为想聊爸爸的事吗？

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:01.940
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah, well, not just because of that, but yeah. I thought that's what you wanted to talk about.
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的，嗯，不只是因为那个，但确实是。我以为那是你想聊的事情。

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:12.420
<v Connor>I have other things on my mind, but hmm, I mean, yeah, that's just a very intense conversation. Can I tell you something crazy that happened today that has nothing to do with him?
<v Connor>我脑子里还有别的事情，但是嗯，我的意思是，那是场非常激烈的对话。我能告诉你今天发生的件疯狂的事吗？跟那个人没关系。

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:38.900
<v Sayre Quevedo>These days, you rarely know where Connor is until he's already there. You call, and he's in Washington with his girlfriend. You call again, and he's barely got service. He's trimming weed in Arcata. Or he's hopped a train to the Midwest. Or he's crashing on a friend's couch in Oakland. Right now, Connor is partying in New Orleans with his girlfriend. Right now, you are pacing around your room in Harlem, staring at a whiteboard where you have scribbled a list of vague to-dos.
<v Sayre Quevedo>这些日子，你很少知道康纳在哪，直到他已经到了那里。你给他打电话，他在华盛顿陪女朋友。再打过去，他信号微弱。他在阿卡塔修剪大麻。或者他跳上一列火车去了中西部。或者他正借住在奥克兰朋友的沙发上。此时此刻，康纳正和他的女朋友在新奥尔良狂欢。此刻，你正在哈莱姆区的房间里踱步，盯着白板，上面乱涂乱画着一堆模糊的待办事项。

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:52.470
<v Connor>Today, me and Claire were walking to go get my bike, and we walked by this guy who apparently used to live here. We keep walking, and all of a sudden, I hear steps, and I look behind.
<v Connor>今天，我和克莱尔走去取我的自行车，我们路过一个以前住在这儿的人。我们继续走，突然，我听到脚步声，我往后看。

00:04:52.470 --> 00:05:21.990
<v Sayre Quevedo>Connor does not like it when you point out your differences, but contrast is the rightful and unavoidable dominion of twins. There are superficial distinctions, the kind of differences strangers point out when you inevitably pull up a photo of Connor after they learn you're a twin-- your tightly coiled curls, Connor's loose, wavy hair; Connor's growth spurt at 16; you're 5' 7, which you say is 5' 8. But nothing makes you feel more different from your brother than when he tells you these kinds of stories.
<v Sayre Quevedo>康纳不喜欢你指出你们之间的差异，但对比是双胞胎理所应当且不可避免的领域。有一些肤浅的区别，比如当你告诉陌生人你是双胞胎后，他们不可避免地调出康纳的照片时指出的那些差异——你紧卷的卷发，康纳松散的波浪发；康纳16岁时经历的生长突增；你身高5英尺7英寸，虽然你说你5英尺8英寸。但当他告诉你这些故事时，没有什么是比这让你感觉和你兄弟如此不同的了。

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:27.110
<v Connor>And I turn around, and the fucking guy is a block away from us--
<v Connor>我转过身，那个混蛋离我们就一个街区远——

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:28.590
<v Sayre Quevedo>Whoa.
<v Sayre Quevedo>哇。

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:35.310
<v Connor>--and just had fucking these knives in his hand and polishing his knives on his shirt and looking at us.
<v Connor>——他手里拿着刀，正在衬衫上擦拭他的刀，盯着我们看。

00:05:35.310 --> 00:06:18.670
<v Sayre Quevedo>The critical difference between you and Connor is this-- long ago, without realizing it, you chose a kind of freedom, the kind that imparts stability and safety, and that you pay for in unsubsidized loans, quarterly job reviews, happy hours with coworkers, boring party conversation, conferences with field practitioners, mid-century modern furniture, boxing classes, and dental checkups. And Connor chose a kind of freedom that comes with land projects, one-off jobs at biker festivals, wide-open mountain ranges from the Union Pacific line, and that he pays for in a persistent smoker's cough, weeks on remote weed farms with Phish fans, and being pursued in New Orleans by a guy with a knife.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你和康纳之间最关键的区别在于——很久以前，你不知不觉地选择了某种自由，那种能带来稳定和安全的自由，你为此付出的代价是未经补贴的贷款、季度工作评估、与同事的欢乐时光、无聊的派对闲谈、行业会议、中世纪现代风格家具、拳击课和牙科检查。而康纳选择了一种自由，伴随着土地项目、机车节上的临时工作、从联合太平洋铁路延伸开来的广阔山脉，他为此付出的代价是挥之不去的吸烟者咳嗽、在偏远大麻农场与 Phish 乐队粉丝度过的几周，以及在新奥尔良被一个持刀男子追赶。

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:25.550
<v Connor>It was just like psycho ass move. So yeah, that was crazy. That's why I was talking to you.
<v Connor>这简直就像那种精神病般的举动。所以，嗯，太疯狂了。这就是为什么我跟你说这个。

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:48.820
<v Sayre Quevedo>Your brother thinks you are a judgmental normie, and you think he is a judgmental hobo. This is the shared cost of your divergent freedoms. You both pay for your choices with the degree of distance it puts between you, but you do love each other, and you are trying to be better, to be nicer, to be closer. This routine of phone calls, which began a few months ago, is your attempt.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你兄弟认为你是个爱评头论足的“正常人”，而你认为他是个爱评头论足的流浪汉。这是你们各自不同的自由所共同付出的代价。你们都用彼此之间产生的距离感来支付各自的选择，但你们确实相爱，并且都在努力变得更好、更友善、更亲近。这种几个月前开始的通话习惯，就是你们的尝试。

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:51.560
<v Sayre Quevedo>Drink water and eat meals.
<v Sayre Quevedo>多喝水，按时吃饭。

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:52.440
<v Connor>Yeah, we have a house.
<v Connor>是的，我们有房子。

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:53.080
<v Sayre Quevedo>All right, I love you.
<v Sayre Quevedo>好了，我爱你。

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:54.620
<v Connor>We can cook and stuff. All right.
<v Connor>我们可以做饭之类的。好的。

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:55.320
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah, I know you're not--
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的，我知道你不是——

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:56.200
<v Connor>I'll see you later.
<v Connor>回头见。

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.640
<v Sayre Quevedo>--eating over a trash fire. OK, I love you.
<v Sayre Quevedo>——在垃圾火堆上吃饭。好的，我爱你。

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:01.440
<v Connor>OK. There's nothing wrong with that.
<v Connor>好的。那没什么问题。

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:14.960
<v Sayre Quevedo>You do not return to the subject of your birth father tonight. There are no records of phone calls with Connor that week or the next. You assume his impulse to look for your father is a passing one. You doubt you follow up.
<v Sayre Quevedo>今晚你们没有回到关于你生父的话题。那一周或下一周都没有与康纳的通话记录。你认为他想寻找你父亲的冲动是一时的。你怀疑你是否会跟进。

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:40.640
<v Sayre Quevedo>You went through your phase, of course-- the one that you imagine most fatherless teenagers go through-- in which your father's absence took on an outsized significance, in which your adolescent brain dissected and disassembled its meaning in relation to your sense of self. Was your fatherlessness why you felt so apart from others? Was his abandonment a sort of permanent wound on your psyche? Only one way to find out-- write a lot of shitty poetry.
<v Sayre Quevedo>当然，你经历过你的阶段——你想象中大多数没有父亲的青少年都会经历的阶段——那时候你父亲的缺席显得意义重大，你青春期的大脑解析并拆解了其对你自我意识的意义。你感到与他人格格不入是因为没有父亲吗？他的遗弃是否对你的心理造成了某种永久性的创伤？只有一个方法可以弄清楚——写一堆烂诗。

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:42.040
<v Sayre Quevedo>[APPLAUSE]
<v Sayre Quevedo>[掌声]

00:07:42.040 --> 00:08:04.090
<v Sayre Quevedo>None of those poems are recorded, thank God. But one vestige invested remains-- a play about two boys waiting to meet their father at a cafe. You wrote it when you were 17. It was called-- perhaps a little overprecisely-- "Waiting to Meet Your Father" and was haltingly, painfully acted out by two fellow art students.
<v Sayre Quevedo>谢天上帝，那些诗都没有被录下来。但留下来一个残迹——一部关于两个男孩在咖啡馆等待见他们父亲的戏。你17岁时写的。它叫——也许有点过于精确地——《等待见你的父亲》，由两名艺术系同学结结巴巴、痛苦地演了出来。

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:05.810
<v Student 1>What if he doesn't show?
<v Student 1>如果他不出现怎么办？

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:06.650
<v Student 2>He will.
<v Student 2>他会来的。

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:08.850
<v Student 1>And if he doesn't?
<v Student 1>如果他不来呢？

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:14.890
<v Student 2>I'll be OK.
<v Student 2>我会没事的。

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:34.050
<v Sayre Quevedo>The central tension of the play is that the two boys are waiting at a cafe to meet their father, who they've never known, but he is running late, or maybe he is standing them up. The brothers argue about whether or not they should wait to find out which one it is. One boy is sure it won't be worth it. The other is adamant they stay.
<v Sayre Quevedo>这部戏的核心冲突是，两个男孩在咖啡馆等待见他们从未谋面的父亲，但他迟到了，或者可能他放了他们鸽子。兄弟俩争论是否应该等下去以弄清楚是哪种情况。一个男孩肯定那不值得。另一个坚持要留下。

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:35.690
<v Student 1>Do you think about him a lot?
<v Student 1>你经常想他吗？

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:37.890
<v Student 2>No. Do you?
<v Student 2>不。你呢？

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:58.650
<v Student 1>Well, sometimes, like when we're about to fall asleep. It's usually while I'm not thinking that I think about him the most. It's one of those things where when my mind is just drifting, I'll suddenly start wondering about him, and then I'll have to make myself do something to take my mind off him. The more I think about trying to think about him, the more I end up thinking about him.
<v Student 1>嗯，有时，比如当我们快睡着的时候。通常是在我不去想的时候，我反而最常想到他。这就是那种当你思绪漂移时，突然开始怀疑他，然后你不得不强迫自己做点什么来转移注意力的情况。你越是刻意去想如何去想他，你最终就越是会想到他。

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:00.370
<v Sayre Quevedo>In the end--
<v Sayre Quevedo>最后——

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:02.210
<v Student 1>Let's go.
<v Student 1>走吧。

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:03.410
<v Student 2>Are you sure?
<v Student 2>你确定吗？

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:05.010
<v Student 1>Yeah.
<v Student 1>是的。

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:08.810
<v Sayre Quevedo>--their father arrives a few moments after they walk out the door.
<v Sayre Quevedo>——在他们走出门口几分钟后，他们的父亲到了。

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:13.970
<v Sayre Quevedo>[APPLAUSE]
<v Sayre Quevedo>[掌声]

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:40.170
<v Sayre Quevedo>You turn 20. On the eve of this new decade of life, you decided it was not worth it to actively think about your birth father anymore. It was not cool to still feel hurt about it. And so you could choose to feel nothing, and you plan to continue to do so now. You will not sabotage your brother's efforts, but you will not help them either. So you do not follow up with Connor about looking for your father. And Connor does not call.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你20岁了。在这个人生新阶段的前夕，你决定不再主动去想你的生父是不值得的。因为这件事仍然感到受伤并不酷。所以你可以选择什么都不感觉，并且计划现在继续这样做。你不会破坏你兄弟的努力，但你也不会帮忙。所以你没有再跟进康纳关于寻找你父亲的事。而康纳也没有再打电话。

00:09:40.170 --> 00:10:00.500
<v Sayre Quevedo>But there are also no records of other calls either. If you had recorded everything, you would hear three sessions here with your new work-subsidized tele-therapist, the first an intake session, a concise, summarized biography of your life as it relates to your inner problems. You make radio documentaries. You won some awards for this last year.
<v Sayre Quevedo>但也没有其他通话记录。如果你录下了一切，你会在这里听到你与新的单位补贴远程治疗师进行的三次会谈，第一次是评估会谈，简明扼要地总结了你的人生及其与内心问题的联系。你制作广播纪录片。去年你还因此得了一些奖。

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:09.130
<v Sayre Quevedo>Whew. Wow. Hold on, one second. There are two parts to this speech, and I was going to keep it short, but what the hell, I got gold, so yeah. [LAUGHS]
<v Sayre Quevedo>呼。哇。等一下，一秒钟。这个演讲有两部分，我本来想长话短说，但管他呢，我拿到了金奖，所以是的。[笑声]

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:09.980
<v Sayre Quevedo>[CHEERING, APPLAUSE]
<v Sayre Quevedo>[欢呼，掌声]

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:24.500
<v Announcer>You crave the attention and acknowledgment, and it also repulses you. You work at the most famous newspaper in the world at one of the biggest shows in the country. Lately, you've been leaving early to see if anyone notices. They don't.
<v Announcer>你渴望关注和认可，但它也让你反感。你在世界上最著名的报纸和国内最大的节目之一工作。最近，你一直提前离开，看看有没有人注意到。他们没有。

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:27.740
<v Child>Ah, alligator!
<v Child>啊，鳄鱼！

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:30.940
<v Sayre Quevedo>You were raised by a loving single mother in the Bay Area.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你在湾区由一位充满爱心的单身母亲抚养长大。

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:33.210
<v Woman>Yikes.
<v Woman>天呐。

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:34.970
<v Child>Alligator.
<v Child>鳄鱼。

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:38.020
<v Woman>Yeah, you've got to be careful. There's alligators here.
<v Woman>是的，你得小心。这里有鳄鱼。

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:54.780
<v Sayre Quevedo>You would hear yourself tell your tele-therapist that your birth father left before you were born, and you've never met him, that you suspect based on nothing more than an elementary understanding of Freud and a few Instagram therapy videos that this is the root of some of your problems-- daddy issues.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你会听到自己在电话里告诉远程治疗师，你的生父在你出生前就离开了，你从未见过他，你怀疑——基于对弗洛伊德最基础的理解和几段 Instagram 心理疗法视频，这正是你某些问题的根源——恋父情结。

00:10:54.780 --> 00:11:08.140
<v Sayre Quevedo>You have a twin brother, but you're not identical. You probably make the same joke you always do, that you're sorry to disappoint your therapist. People are always more excited when they think there's some other version of you out there.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你有一个双胞胎兄弟，但不是同卵。你可能会开你一贯的那个玩笑，说你很抱歉让你的治疗师失望了。当人们认为外面还有一个版本的你时，通常会更兴奋。

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:47.470
<v Sayre Quevedo>Most relevant to your tele-therapist, what you would like to fix about yourself-- it is mostly one thing, which you are almost embarrassed to admit-- you have the sneaking suspicion that you will never fall in love. Your last attempt officially ended the night of your 27th birthday, you and your ex shouting at one another beneath the rattling viaduct of the M line, your ex saying it's been two years. You need to get over me. I got over you in a week. I know, you said, I know-- even though you didn't know.
<v Sayre Quevedo>对你的远程治疗师来说最相关的是，你想修补自己的什么——主要是一件事，你几乎不好意思承认——你有一种隐秘的怀疑，你永远不会坠入爱河。你的最后一次尝试正式结束于你27岁生日那天晚上，你和前任在 M 线铁轨下嘎吱作响的桥下大声争吵，你的前任说已经两年了。你需要放下我。我在一周内就放下你了。我知道，你说，我知道——尽管你根本不知道。

00:11:47.470 --> 00:12:06.550
<v Sayre Quevedo>That is as far as you get. Your therapist calls it quits at the start of the pandemic. You build fortresses of routine to keep the mania of your confinement from creeping in. You quit smoking, start running. You record your phone calls. You tell yourself it's for a project you hope to make one day about this historic moment.
<v Sayre Quevedo>这就是你所能达到的极限。疫情开始时，你的治疗师退出了。你筑起日常生活的堡垒，防止禁闭带来的狂躁潜入。你戒了烟，开始跑步。你录下你的通话。你告诉自己这是为了一个你希望有一天能为这个历史性时刻制作的项目。

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:08.430
<v Michelle>Hi, honey. How are you?
<v Michelle>嗨，宝贝。你好吗？

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:09.870
<v Sayre Quevedo>Hello.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你好。

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:14.430
<v Announcer>Your aunt and your mom have been living together in Portland since quarantine began.
<v Announcer>自隔离开始以来，你阿姨和你妈妈一直住在波特兰。

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:15.170
<v Mom>I'm back.
<v Mom>我回来了。

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:16.330
<v Michelle>Your mommy's back. She's here.
<v Michelle>你妈妈回来了。她在这儿。

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:16.870
<v Sayre Quevedo>You're back.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你回来了。

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:17.750
<v Michelle>We're both here.
<v Michelle>我们俩都在这儿。

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:24.870
<v Mom>So does it go from bear to otter, and then to something else? Or it's just bear--
<v Mom>那么它是从熊（bear）变成水獭（otter），然后再变成别的什么吗？还是说这只是熊——

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:29.470
<v Sayre Quevedo>It's not an evolution. It's just different groups.
<v Sayre Quevedo>这不是进化。这只是不同的群体。

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:29.870
<v Michelle>OK.
<v Michelle>好的。

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:33.070
<v Mom>Twinks are a totally different category, right?
<v Mom>小鲜肉（Twinks）是一个完全不同的类别，对吧？

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:53.190
<v Sayre Quevedo>This is your favorite kind of call. You get stoned and curl up in bed with the phone balanced on your cheek. You are still, at 27, like most children. You like to hear stories of the world before you were in it of your mother before she was your mother, like when her and your aunt were strippers at the New Century Theater in San Francisco.
<v Sayre Quevedo>这是你最喜欢的那种通话。你抽着大麻，蜷缩在床上，电话贴在脸颊上。27岁的你，依然像大多数孩子一样。你喜欢听你在出生前的世界的故事，听你母亲在你出生前作为你母亲的故事，比如当她和你阿姨在旧金山新世纪剧院当脱衣舞女的时候。

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:56.990
<v Sayre Quevedo>And what did you dance to? And what did my mom dance to?
<v Sayre Quevedo>你们跳舞的伴奏是什么？我妈妈跳舞的伴奏是什么？

00:12:56.990 --> 00:13:01.430
<v Michelle>I would dance to Bob Dylan a lot, actually, believe it or not. But so did your mom.
<v Michelle>其实，信不信由你，我经常伴着鲍勃·迪伦跳舞。你妈妈也是。

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:14.750
<v Mom>Well, yeah, but his Christian album. I'm going to scandalize your nephew now. Your auntie did the schoolgirl thing. Let's just be honest. Let's just be honest. Yeah.
<v Mom>嗯，是的，但那是他的基督徒专辑。我现在要让你表弟感到震惊了。你阿姨当时跳的是校服诱惑风。我们老实说吧。我们老实说吧。是的。

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:15.870
<v Michelle>Yes.
<v Michelle>是的。

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:19.710
<v Sayre Quevedo>Creepy, but I won't think about that.
<v Sayre Quevedo>毛骨悚然，但我不会去想那个。

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:23.190
<v Michelle>I know. When you think about it, super creepy.
<v Michelle>我知道。当你想到它的时候，超级毛骨悚然。

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:31.030
<v Mom>Well, I'm warning you that my phone is-- you know what? I'm going to go upstairs and get the cord. But I have to look at this message from your brother.
<v Mom>好吧，我警告你我的手机是——你知道吗？我要上楼去拿充电线。但我得看看你兄弟发来的这条信息。

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:36.800
<v Michelle>OK, now's your chance, Sayre. Your mom's going upstairs. [LAUGHS] No, go ahead.
<v Michelle>好了，现在是你的机会，塞尔。[笑声] 不，去吧。

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:48.280
<v Sayre Quevedo>What, um-- what is her-- what are her-- oh, did you ever meet my birth father?
<v Sayre Quevedo>什么，嗯——她是什么——她们是什么——哦，你见过我的生父吗？

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:49.880
<v Michelle>Yeah.
<v Michelle>见过。

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:53.120
<v Sayre Quevedo>And what was your general impression?
<v Sayre Quevedo>你对他有什么印象？

00:13:53.120 --> 00:14:09.720
<v Michelle>He was OK. I only met your birth father a couple of times. I remember I met him once at the Zeitgeist, and he was really hella tall for one thing. He was cute. He was handsome.
<v Michelle>他还好。我只见过你生父几次。我记得有一次在 Zeitgeist 酒吧见的，首先他个子非常高。他很可爱。他很帅。

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:35.760
<v Sayre Quevedo>Your mother is not exactly withholding about your father. She answers the questions if you ask them, which you did a lot when you were a teenager. Your origin story has remained consistent but unsatisfyingly brief. Your father was a line cook. He played in a rockabilly band. They met at a bar called the Zeitgeist, dated for a while. She got pregnant. He wasn't ready to be a father. She decided to keep you.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你母亲对于你父亲的事并不是那种遮遮掩掩的人。如果你问她，她会回答，你十几岁时确实经常问。你的起源故事一直保持一致，但极其简短且令人不满足。你父亲是个厨师。他在一支摇滚乐团演奏。他们在一家叫 Zeitgeist 的酒吧相遇，约会了一阵子。她怀孕了。他还没准备好当父亲。她决定把你生下来。

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:49.440
<v Sayre Quevedo>For a while, she exchanged letters with his mother, but your mom says she was always justifying his choice to not be involved in your lives, so they stopped communicating. Your mother tells you his name, but says she can't remember how it's spelled.
<v Sayre Quevedo>有一段时间，她和你父亲的母亲交换过信件，但你妈妈说，她当时总是在为他选择不参与你们的生活做辩护，所以她们停止了联系。你母亲告诉了你他的名字，但说她记不清怎么拼写了。

00:14:49.440 --> 00:15:06.400
<v Michelle>So yeah. So I met him a couple of times. I thought-- and he seemed nice. He was nice to me. He was polite. He was clearly trying to make a good impression on me because I was your mom's friend. Yeah, and he was very tall.
<v Michelle>所以，是的。我见过他几次。我认为——他看起来很不错。他对我很友好。他很礼貌。很明显他试图给我留下好印象，因为我是你妈妈的朋友。是的，而且他非常高。

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:23.280
<v Sayre Quevedo>The pandemic inspires inexplicable life choices. Some friends buzz their hair. Others experiment with sobriety. You move to Los Angeles. You are surprised to find that your next-door neighbors, a young white couple, rarely return your greetings.
<v Sayre Quevedo>疫情激发了一些莫名其妙的人生选择。一些朋友把头发剪成了平头。其他人则尝试禁欲。你搬到了洛杉矶。你惊讶地发现你的隔壁邻居，一对年轻的白人夫妇，很少回应你的问候。

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:50.170
<v Sayre Quevedo>You live in the heart of a gentrifying Central American neighborhood. When you speak to the cashier at the corner store in Spanish, she responds in English. You want to tell her that you're not like the rest of them. Your grandmother's from Santa Ana. You couldn't have afforded to live here a few years ago. You moved here to be closer to something. You're just not sure what or how.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你住在一个正在中产阶级化的中美洲社区中心。当你在街角商店用西班牙语和收银员说话时，她用英语回应。你想告诉她你和他们不一样。你祖母来自圣安娜。几年前你本来根本住不起这里。你搬到这里是为了更接近某样东西。只是你不确定是什么，或者是怎么回事。

00:15:50.170 --> 00:16:20.210
<v Sayre Quevedo>Connor is driving his truck up and down the Pacific Coast Highway. He's waiting to be called in to the wildfires, but so far, nobody has called, and he spent the summer sleeping in his truck. On April 18, 2021, a little over a year after he first calls you to ask to look for your father, he texts you a series of screenshots. They're of an exchange between him and someone from a DNA testing website, a distant relative. You bought a kit for your family a few years ago and forgot about it.
<v Sayre Quevedo>康纳开着他的卡车在太平洋海岸公路上来回行驶。他在等候被征召去扑灭野火，但目前为止，没有人给他打电话，整个夏天他都在卡车里睡觉。2021年4月18日，在他第一次打电话给你询问寻找你父亲的事一年多后，他给你发了一系列截图。那是他和一个 DNA 测试网站上的某个人——一个远房亲戚——之间的交流。几年前你给家人买过一个工具包，然后就忘了这回事。

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:44.580
<v Sayre Quevedo>In the exchange, Connor asks if there's someone in this distant relative's family with your birth father's name. Yes, in fact, says the stranger. A few days later, Connor receives your birth father's first and last name, for the first time, spelled correctly. When you search your birth father's name in public records, it returns a result in Los Angeles, in a suburb 40 minutes away from you.
<v Sayre Quevedo>在交流中，康纳询问这个远房亲戚的家族里是否有一个和你生父名字相同的人。事实上是的，陌生人说。几天后，康纳第一次收到了你生父准确拼写的全名。当你在公共记录中搜索你生父的名字时，结果显示他在洛杉矶，在离你40分钟路程的一个郊区。

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:47.100
<v Connor>I mean, how do you feel about it?
<v Connor>我的意思是，你对此有什么感觉？

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:50.780
<v Sayre Quevedo>I feel good about it. Yeah, I feel good about it.
<v Sayre Quevedo>感觉不错。是的，感觉不错。

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:58.780
<v Connor>For me, the first day that we got that info, I was like, I'm kind of excited.
<v Connor>对我来说，得到那个消息的第一天，我就想，我有点兴奋。

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:18.300
<v Sayre Quevedo>It has been a long time since you've heard Connor excited about anything. It's sweet and unnerving. To say no would be disappointing for him, a betrayal of your attempts towards closeness. But the thought of meeting your birth father inspires very little feeling in you, besides a nascent hope that you might finally finish a project.
<v Sayre Quevedo>康纳已经很久没有对任何事情感到兴奋了。这种感觉既甜蜜又令人不安。说“不”会让他失望，背叛了你们走向亲近的尝试。但想到要见到生父，除了内心萌生的一丝希望——你可能终于能完成一个项目之外，并没有给你带来太多的触动。

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:25.900
<v Connor>I mean, do you feel like if you met him, do you feel like you won't get anything out of it?
<v Connor>我的意思是，你觉得如果见到他，你觉得你什么也得不到吗？

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:37.780
<v Sayre Quevedo>I guess I'm just trying to figure out what-- I feel like we're both almost 30, where it's just like, what is this guy going to tell us about ourselves that we don't already know?
<v Sayre Quevedo>我想我只是试图弄清楚什么——我觉得我们都快30岁了，感觉就像是，这个家伙能告诉我们什么关于我们自己，是我们还不知道的？

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:59.340
<v Connor>I hear you because it's like I'm not interested-- it's like I don't really give a shit about-- I mean, I do give a shit. I do give a shit about meeting him. I don't know. Do you ever look in the mirror and, say, think about Dad, or Dad, but our biological dad?
<v Connor>我听到了，因为感觉我也不感兴趣——我的意思是，我并不真的在乎——我的意思是，我在乎。我确实在乎见到他。我不知道。你有没有看着镜子，然后想到爸爸，或者说，爸爸，但我们的亲生父亲？

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:02.620
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah. Yeah, definitely.
<v Sayre Quevedo>有。是的，绝对有。

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:21.580
<v Connor>It's like, I find myself always looking and being like, I don't even know what our family looks like. I have Mom to compare that to. I'm like, oh, it's like I have mom's face, in a lot of ways. But I also wonder what our biological dad looks like.
<v Connor>这就像，我发现自己总是看着，心里想，我甚至不知道我们的家人长什么样。我有妈妈可以对比。我想，哦，看起来我有妈妈的脸，在很多方面。但我也想知道我们的亲生父亲长什么样。

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:40.270
<v Sayre Quevedo>You have tabs open to several potential fathers on Facebook, a series of white men in rimless or squared square sunglasses with varied hairlines and gradient palette shades. Your top contender sports a buzz cut and a pair of gas station sunglasses. He's an amateur photographer who takes pictures of animals at the zoo.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你在 Facebook 上打开了几个潜在父亲的标签页，一系列戴着无框或方形太阳镜的白人男子，发际线各异，色调阴影不一。你排名最高的候选人留着寸头，戴着加油站买的那种太阳镜。他是一名业余摄影师，专门拍动物园里的动物。

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:45.830
<v Sayre Quevedo>I found one picture of a person who kind of looks like he could be related to us maybe.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我发现了一张照片，那个人看起来可能和我们有血缘关系，也许。

00:18:45.830 --> 00:18:50.550
<v Connor>Yeah, I saw one that maybe did. But I was like, damn, he's pretty ugly. I don't know.
<v Connor>是的，我也看到了一张，也许有。但我当时想，该死，他长得真丑。我不知道。

00:18:50.550 --> 00:18:53.990
<v Sayre Quevedo>Is it the guy with the black hair and the sunglasses on?
<v Sayre Quevedo>是那个留着黑发，戴着太阳镜的人吗？

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:59.910
<v Connor>Yeah, that guy. I was like, is that really Mom's type before she started dating really boring dudes?
<v Connor>是的，那个家伙。我当时想，那真的是妈妈在开始和那些很无聊的家伙约会之前的类型吗？

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:09.950
<v Sayre Quevedo>You have not told your mother about any of this, and you're not sure you will. It is very different, you think, to ask questions about your birth father than to meet him.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你还没有告诉母亲任何关于这些的事，而且你不确定你会不会说。你认为询问关于生父的问题和真正见到他是两码事。

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:14.430
<v Connor>And I'm curious about what his life is at.
<v Connor>而且我很好奇他的生活怎么样。

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:16.230
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah.
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的。

00:19:16.230 --> 00:19:24.750
<v Connor>I also hope he's balling. [LAUGHS] No, I'm just kidding.
<v Connor>我还希望他很有钱。[笑声] 不，我在开玩笑。

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:43.470
<v Sayre Quevedo>Suddenly, it's June again, almost exactly one year since you arrived to Los Angeles. One night, you will absentmindedly google your father's name and Los Angeles. The screen will populate with the same buzzcut guy with gas station sunglasses, but now there are two new images.
<v Sayre Quevedo>突然间，又是六月了，距离你到达洛杉矶几乎正好一年。一天晚上，你心不在焉地搜索了你父亲的名字和洛杉矶。屏幕上弹出了同一个留着寸头、戴着加油站太阳镜的家伙，但现在多了两张新图片。

00:19:43.470 --> 00:20:05.630
<v Sayre Quevedo>The first is a man standing beside a barber chair. He looks like he was ripped from a Time magazine article from the '50s-- tall, a pencil mustache, slicked-back hair. There's an interview with him. He owns the barber shop. He grew up in California, moved to Los Angeles in the mid '90s. Before he was a barber, he says, he was a cook.
<v Sayre Quevedo>第一张是一个男人站在理发椅旁。他看起来就像是从50年代的《时代》杂志文章里撕下来的一样——高个子，留着铅笔胡，头发向后梳。有一篇对他的采访。他拥有这家理发店。他在加州长大，90年代中期搬到了洛杉矶。他说，在成为理发师之前，他是一名厨师。

00:20:05.630 --> 00:20:46.160
<v Sayre Quevedo>The second image is of him standing in front of the shop. It looks familiar. You will type the address into Google Maps. You ate dinner across the street from the shop two nights ago. You will type in your address to measure the distance between you and this barber shop. A short, nearly straight line will appear and an estimated arrival time from your apartment-- five minutes.
<v Sayre Quevedo>第二张图片是他站在店前。看起来很眼熟。你会把地址输入谷歌地图。两天前你刚在那家店对面吃过晚饭。你会输入你的地址来测量你和这家理发店之间的距离。一条简短、几乎是直线的路径会出现，从你公寓出发的预估到达时间——五分钟。

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:52.420
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, some decisions that need to be made. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，有些决定需要做。留下来，和我们在一起。

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:57.890
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. Our story today from Sayre Quevedo continues.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。今天我们的故事来自塞尔·克韦多，继续进行。

00:20:57.890 --> 00:21:06.720
<v Ira Glass>[PHONE DIALING]
<v Ira Glass>[电话拨号声]

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:08.080
