00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:49.740
<v Sayre Quevedo>You find an email address on the barber shop's website. You click into the always-open email tab on your computer and start a new message. Your first words to your birth father will forever be archived in your inbox between updates to your credit score, calendar reminders of work deadlines, and your lease renewal.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你在理发店的网站上找到了一个电子邮箱。你点击电脑上一直开着的邮件标签页，开始写一封新邮件。你对生父说的第一句话将永远归档在你的收件箱里，夹在你的信用评分更新、工作截止日期的日历提醒和租赁续约合同之间。

00:30:49.740 --> 00:31:07.260
<v Sayre Quevedo>The cursor ticks an even measure on the blank draft. This blank space must be filled with something specific. A particular alchemy must take place, a certain tone struck. Whatever fills this space must be firm but not aggressive, humble but not shrinking.
<v Sayre Quevedo>光标在空白的草稿上均匀地跳动。这个空白处必须填入具体的东西。某种化学反应必须发生，某种语调必须确立。填补这个空间的任何东西都必须坚定但不激进，谦逊但不卑微。

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:09.900
<v Connor>Do you have any idea how to start?
<v Connor>你知道怎么开始吗？

00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:30.980
<v Sayre Quevedo>How about, Hello. I know this is a bit out of the blue, but I'm sending this message on behalf of my brother and I. We think you are our biological father.
<v Sayre Quevedo>怎么样，你好。我知道这有点突然，但我代表我和我兄弟发这条信息。我们认为你是我们的亲生父亲。

00:31:30.980 --> 00:31:36.460
<v Connor>Start with that preface thing you're talking about. We're not looking to get anything from you.
<v Connor>从你说的那个前言开始。我们不是要从你那得到什么。

00:31:36.460 --> 00:31:43.780
<v Sayre Quevedo>How about this? I know this is a bit out of the blue, but I'm sending this message on behalf of my brother and I. We think you are our biological father. We're not--
<v Sayre Quevedo>这个怎么样？我知道这有点突然，但我代表我和我兄弟发这条信息。我们认为你是我们的亲生父亲。我们不是——

00:31:43.780 --> 00:31:46.700
<v Connor>That sounds so formal and weird.
<v Connor>听起来太正式、太奇怪了。

00:31:46.700 --> 00:31:50.900
<v Sayre Quevedo>We don't need anything from you. We don't-- we're not asking for anything.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我们不需要你什么。我们不——我们没要求什么。

00:31:50.900 --> 00:31:52.480
<v Connor>I might want to ask him for something.
<v Connor>我可能想问他要点什么。

00:31:52.480 --> 00:31:55.340
<v Connor>[LAUGHTER]
<v Connor>[笑声]

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:58.580
<v Connor>I'll be like, hey, can you pay for my schooling?
<v Connor>我会说，嘿，你能付我的学费吗？

00:31:58.580 --> 00:32:04.070
<v Sayre Quevedo>Connor, please don't ruin this before we've even had the chance to meet him. Please.
<v Sayre Quevedo>康纳，拜托不要在我们有机会见到他之前就把它搞砸了。拜托。

00:32:04.070 --> 00:32:27.150
<v Connor>I won't. I won't. Yeah, it still sounds formal. It still sounds like what are you-- We'd love to talk, blah, blah, blah. But it could be like, we think that you might be our biological father. Whatever. We're really hoping that we could meet you and fill in the blank, something personal.
<v Connor>不会的。不会的。是的，还是听起来很正式。还是听起来像你是什么——我们很想谈谈，巴拉巴拉。但它可以是，我们认为你可能是我们的亲生父亲。不管怎样。我们真的很希望我们能见到你，填补空白，一些私人的东西。

00:32:27.150 --> 00:32:31.380
<v Sayre Quevedo>We're both nearly 30--
<v Sayre Quevedo>我们都快30岁了——

00:32:31.380 --> 00:32:31.830
<v Connor>Yeah.
<v Connor>是的。

00:32:31.830 --> 00:32:38.470
<v Sayre Quevedo>--and have always wondered about who you were.
<v Sayre Quevedo>——而且一直好奇你是谁。

00:32:38.470 --> 00:32:42.150
<v Connor>Yeah, that sounds good to me. Does that sound good to you?
<v Connor>是的，这对我也很受用。这对你来说听起来不错吗？

00:32:42.150 --> 00:32:44.630
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah, I'm sending it.
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的，我正在发送。

00:32:44.630 --> 00:32:45.950
<v Connor>All right.
<v Connor>好的。

00:32:45.950 --> 00:32:47.750
<v Sayre Quevedo>Ready? 3--
<v Sayre Quevedo>准备好了吗？3——

00:32:47.750 --> 00:32:57.470
<v Connor>Yeah. Smash that motherfucking button. Sorry. Yeah. I'm ready. You can count down if you want.
<v Connor>是的。按下那个该死的按钮。抱歉。是的。我准备好了。你想倒数的话就倒数吧。

00:32:57.470 --> 00:32:59.490
<v Sayre Quevedo>What should I put as the subject line?
<v Sayre Quevedo>主题行我该写什么？

00:32:59.490 --> 00:33:04.470
<v Connor>Say, like, Happy Father's day.
<v Connor>写，比如，父亲节快乐。

00:33:04.470 --> 00:33:10.630
<v Sayre Quevedo>Papa, can you hear me? No. Are you my daddy?
<v Sayre Quevedo>爸爸，你能听见我吗？不。你是我的爸爸吗？

00:33:10.630 --> 00:33:17.790
<v Connor>How about long lost kids in search of biological father.
<v Connor>长失散的孩子寻找亲生父亲怎么样。

00:33:17.790 --> 00:33:21.070
<v Sayre Quevedo>That's a long subject, not just--
<v Sayre Quevedo>这是一个很长的主题，不仅仅是——

00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:22.810
<v Connor>Dad, question mark?
<v Connor>爸爸，问号？

00:33:22.810 --> 00:33:27.750
<v Sayre Quevedo>I'm just going to put a hello from Connor and Sayre. How about that?
<v Sayre Quevedo>我只是要写一个来自康纳和塞尔的问候。那个怎么样？

00:33:27.750 --> 00:33:32.270
<v Connor>Reaching out.
<v Connor>接触。

00:33:32.270 --> 00:33:39.350
<v Sayre Quevedo>All right, 1, 2, 3. All right, I did it.
<v Sayre Quevedo>好的，1, 2, 3。好的，我做了。

00:33:39.350 --> 00:33:45.950
<v Connor>Nice. Now we wait.
<v Connor>很好。现在我们等。

00:33:45.950 --> 00:34:10.960
<v Ira Glass>Our story continues after a break from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues. It's This American Life. We are in the middle of excerpts from Sayre Quevedo's new series Reaching Out. Let's pick up where we left off.
<v Ira Glass>我们的故事在芝加哥公共广播电台休息后继续，当我们的节目继续时。这里是《美国生活》。我们正在节目中摘录塞尔·克韦多的新系列《接触》。让我们从刚才结束的地方接着讲。

00:34:10.960 --> 00:34:30.960
<v Sayre Quevedo>You wait four days to hear back, but nothing. There's no way to know the message was received, that it didn't end up in his spam folder or that a coworker didn't delete it assuming it was a phishing scam. But the notion of these possibilities is enough to convince you that it's worth reaching out once more. So you call the shop.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你等了四天期待回音，但什么都没有。没法知道信息是否被收到，没有落入他的垃圾邮件文件夹，或者没有被同事误删以为是钓鱼诈骗。但这些可能性的想法足以让你确信，值得再联系一次。所以你给店里打了电话。

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:32.120
<v Man>Hello?
<v Man>喂？

00:34:32.120 --> 00:34:32.660
<v Sayre Quevedo>Hello.
<v Sayre Quevedo>喂。

00:34:32.660 --> 00:34:33.800
<v Man>Hi.
<v Man>嗨。

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:37.200
<v Sayre Quevedo>He's in the bathroom, says the voice on the other end of the line.
<v Sayre Quevedo>他在洗手间，电话那头的声音说道。

00:34:37.200 --> 00:34:40.080
<v Man>So it's Sayre-- S-A-Y-R-E?
<v Man>所以是塞尔——S-A-Y-R-E？

00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:43.600
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah, and Connor-- C-O-N-N-O-R.
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的，还有康纳——C-O-N-N-O-R。

00:34:43.600 --> 00:34:45.480
<v Ira Glass>You leave your names and number.
<v Ira Glass>你留下了你们的名字和号码。

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:50.520
<v Sayre Quevedo>Sure. It's 510--
<v Sayre Quevedo>好的。是510——

00:34:50.520 --> 00:34:54.320
<v Connor>What do you think? Do you think he was actually in the bathroom?
<v Connor>你觉得呢？你认为他真的在洗手间吗？

00:34:54.320 --> 00:35:01.720
<v Sayre Quevedo>I don't know. Dude, it sounds-- it's like one of those things that could be true, but it also could be a really lame excuse, and it's hard to know.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我不知道。伙计，听起来——这就像那种可能是真的，但也可以是一个非常烂的借口的事情，很难说。

00:35:01.720 --> 00:35:07.600
<v Connor>Yeah. Well, if he calls you tonight, I guess let me know.
<v Connor>是的。好吧，如果他今晚给你回电话，我想让我知道。

00:35:07.600 --> 00:35:12.040
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah, I will.
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的，我会的。

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:24.760
<v Ira Glass>You don't hear back, and you don't call back either. You and Connor don't even discuss it. It seems to go without saying that to reach out a third time with no response would be pathetic.
<v Ira Glass>你没收到回音，也没再打回去。你和康纳甚至没有讨论过这件事。无需多言似乎就是，第三次联系却没有回应将是可悲的。

00:35:24.760 --> 00:36:07.370
<v Ira Glass>Sometimes in the weeks after, you find yourself driving past your father's shop. You peer through the window, hoping to catch his face. It's not that it particularly matters to you. You've just become habituated to looking for him.
<v Ira Glass>在接下来的几周里，有时你会发现自己开车经过你父亲的店。你透过窗户盯着看，希望能捕捉到他的脸。并不是因为这特别对你重要。你只是习惯了寻找他。

00:36:07.370 --> 00:36:24.930
<v Ira Glass>You've gone out alone again tonight. In the months since quarantine was lifted, this has become your weekend routine. You stay out at your neighborhood bar until close with your back to the wall and your eyes on the door. You're waiting for someone to walk in who will change the course of your evening.
<v Ira Glass>今晚你又独自出去了。自隔离解除以来的几个月里，这成了你的周末日常。你待在社区酒吧直到打烊，背靠着墙，眼睛盯着门。你在等某个人走进来，能改变你今晚进程的人。

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:40.250
<v Ira Glass>Hours pass like this, lost continents floating at the bottom of your pint glass. At an interval of every hour, you ask the universe for a sign that it's time to go home. But if it's sent, you are too busy watching the door to notice.
<v Ira Glass>时光就这样流逝，迷失的大陆漂浮在你的品脱杯底。每过一小时，你就向宇宙请求一个该回家了的信号。但如果它发出了，你正忙着盯着门看而没有注意到。

00:36:40.250 --> 00:37:01.890
<v Sayre Quevedo>All right. I am walking home. I'm drunk.
<v Sayre Quevedo>好了。我正在走路回家。我喝醉了。

00:37:01.890 --> 00:37:08.130
<v Ira Glass>Last call is over. Now you're stumbling home, feeling bad for yourself, of course.
<v Ira Glass>最后一次点单结束了。现在你踉踉跄跄地回家，当然，觉得很对不起自己。

00:37:08.130 --> 00:37:30.770
<v Sayre Quevedo>Thought that-- stupidly thought that if I went to the nearby gay bar next to my house that I might meet someone, but no.
<v Sayre Quevedo>以为——愚蠢地以为如果我去了我家附近的同性恋酒吧，也许能遇到某个人，但没有。

00:37:30.770 --> 00:37:53.460
<v Ira Glass>You're on Santa Monica Boulevard now. The leather bar you visit on other nights, sometimes the same night, is to your left. You won't go in tonight. You're already too drunk. The circuit is your Bermuda Triangle, and somewhere along the vertices, you black out. The only evidence of whoever is navigating your body is in these recordings.
<v Ira Glass>你现在在圣莫尼卡大道上。你有时在其他晚上光顾的皮衣酒吧，有时是同一个晚上，就在你的左边。今晚你不会进去。你已经喝得太醉了。这个回路是你的百慕大三角，在顶点的某个地方，你断片了。谁在操纵你的身体的唯一证据就在这些录音里。

00:37:53.460 --> 00:38:05.010
<v Sayre Quevedo>Nothing is nothing. It's all bullshit, all of it.
<v Sayre Quevedo>什么都不是什么。全都是狗屁，全都是。

00:38:05.010 --> 00:38:52.510
<v Ira Glass>To attempt to intuit what bullshit you are referring to from this side of the veil is impossible. Could be anything-- the bullshit of this age, this time, the bullshit of leaving your home only to find that the world is exactly as you left it but with more rules, more distance, more death and indifference. The bullshit of risking disease, waiting for someone to notice you at a bar. The bullshit of knowing that's a waste of time. The bullshit of wanting it anyway. The bullshit of steeling yourself against disappointment by expecting nothing, only to find yourself still disappointed when nothing is exactly what you receive. And then this, whatever this is.
<v Ira Glass>试图从面纱的这一侧去直觉你所指的“狗屁”是什么是不可能的。可能是任何东西——这个时代的狗屁，这个时刻的狗屁，离开家却发现世界一如既往但有着更多规则、更多距离、更多死亡和冷漠的狗屁。冒着疾病风险，在酒吧等着别人注意到你的狗屁。明知那是浪费时间却还是去做这些的狗屁。无论如何都想要它的狗屁。通过期待什么都不发生来防御失望，却发现当真的什么都没发生时你依然感到失望的狗屁。然后这个，不管这是什么。

00:38:52.510 --> 00:38:54.510
<v Sayre Quevedo>I think I'm going to pee.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我想我要尿尿。

00:38:54.510 --> 00:39:06.750
<v Ira Glass>Tomorrow's headache creeping in as you piss behind a tree-- same bullshit, your bullshit, followed by a bean burrito at 7-eleven.
<v Ira Glass>当你在一棵树后撒尿时，明天的头痛感悄然而至——同样的狗屁，你的狗屁，接着是7-11便利店的豆子卷饼。

00:39:06.750 --> 00:39:33.750
<v Sayre Quevedo>Same bullshit every day. Same shit every day. Nothing matters.
<v Sayre Quevedo>每天都是同样的狗屁。每天都是同样的狗屁。什么都不重要。

00:39:33.750 --> 00:39:47.680
<v Ira Glass>Connor and you know there is only one option left. You have to go and meet your father in person. But you haven't talked much since calling your father's shop, and choosing a date is an overture in your conversation with no resolution.
<v Ira Glass>你和康纳知道只剩下最后一个选择。你们必须去亲身见你们的父亲。但自从给你们父亲的店打电话后你们聊得不多，确定一个日期在你们的对话中是一次毫无结果的尝试。

00:39:47.680 --> 00:39:54.950
<v Ira Glass>[PHONE RINGING]
<v Ira Glass>[电话铃声]

00:39:54.950 --> 00:39:56.030
<v Sayre Quevedo>How's it going?
<v Sayre Quevedo>怎么样了？

00:39:56.030 --> 00:39:58.390
<v Connor>What are you doing?
<v Connor>你在干什么？

00:39:58.390 --> 00:40:01.430
<v Sayre Quevedo>Good. I just woke up a bit ago. I'm going to take a shower.
<v Sayre Quevedo>很好。我刚醒没多久。我要去冲个澡。

00:40:01.430 --> 00:40:04.030
<v Connor>Oh, nice. You just woke up?
<v Connor>哦，真好。你刚醒？

00:40:04.030 --> 00:40:05.710
<v Sayre Quevedo>A little bit ago.
<v Sayre Quevedo>刚才一点。

00:40:05.710 --> 00:40:09.230
<v Connor>It's 1:30.
<v Connor>都1点半了。

00:40:09.230 --> 00:40:11.020
<v Sayre Quevedo>I know.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我知道。

00:40:11.020 --> 00:40:13.470
<v Connor>That's crazy.
<v Connor>太疯狂了。

00:40:13.470 --> 00:40:15.910
<v Sayre Quevedo>You never sent me dates.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你从来没发给我日期。

00:40:15.910 --> 00:40:34.590
<v Connor>I know because a bunch of things happened, which is why I was calling you about. Anyway, fuck, dude. I'm really distracted. I'm sorry.
<v Connor>我知道，因为发生了一堆事，所以我才给你打电话的。总之，去你的，伙计。我很分心。我很抱歉。

00:40:34.590 --> 00:40:37.430
<v Sayre Quevedo>I know. Why did you call me while you were doing something else? Do you want to just--
<v Sayre Quevedo>我知道。为什么你在做别的事的时候给我打电话？你想只是——

00:40:37.430 --> 00:40:38.310
<v Connor>You can tell?
<v Connor>你能看出来？

00:40:38.310 --> 00:40:47.210
<v Sayre Quevedo>Just tell me. Yeah, but just-- just-- finish one thing and then do the other. Finish one thing and then do the other. Don't do both at the same time.
<v Sayre Quevedo>直接告诉我就行。是的，但只是——只是——做完一件事再做另一件。做完一件事再做另一件。不要同时做两件事。

00:40:47.210 --> 00:40:47.400
<v Connor>OK.
<v Connor>好的。

00:40:47.400 --> 00:40:51.280
<v Sayre Quevedo>Just choose one thing and then do the other.
<v Sayre Quevedo>选一件事做，然后再做另一件。

00:40:51.280 --> 00:40:53.320
<v Connor>You're grumpy.
<v Connor>你脾气暴躁。

00:40:53.320 --> 00:40:55.680
<v Sayre Quevedo>I am grumpy.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我是脾气暴躁。

00:40:55.680 --> 00:40:58.720
<v Connor>It's not my fault.
<v Connor>这不是我的错。

00:40:58.720 --> 00:41:03.400
<v Sayre Quevedo>It's not your fault, but don't call people while you're doing other things.
<v Sayre Quevedo>不是你的错，但不要在做别的事的时候打电话。

00:41:03.400 --> 00:41:06.780
<v Connor>If you want to talk at a better time when you're less hungover.
<v Connor>如果你想在一个更好的时候聊，等你酒醒一点。

00:41:06.780 --> 00:41:11.660
<v Sayre Quevedo>No, I want to talk. I've been trying to get a date. I've been trying to get a date from you for, like, the last two weeks, so just tell me.
<v Sayre Quevedo>不，我想谈。我一直在试图定个日期。在过去两周里，我一直在试图从你那定个日期，所以直接告诉我。

00:41:11.660 --> 00:41:44.120
<v Connor>I know. Well, Sayre, it's really fucking hard with what I do for work. I don't think you really understand that, and I don't blame you for it. It's just like there's an understanding of seasonal work that I don't think you get. But it's really like-- you just kind of put your life on the line and really hope that you get work. I don't have as much freedom as you in some sense. Like, I probably can't make it down at least until December, which I'm totally OK with doing if you want to. But I also understand.
<v Connor>我知道。嗯，塞尔，考虑到我的工作，这真的很他妈困难。我不觉得你真的理解，我不怪你。这只是——对季节性工作有一种理解，我不觉得你明白。但这真的像是——你把生命押在赌注上，真的希望你能找到工作。在某种意义上，我没有你那么自由。比如，我大概至少要到十二月才能去，如果你想的话，我完全可以。但我也理解。

00:41:44.120 --> 00:41:48.040
<v Sayre Quevedo>I'm going to be-- I'm going to be out of town in December, so I can't do it then.
<v Sayre Quevedo>十二月我要出城，所以那天不行。

00:41:48.040 --> 00:41:51.720
<v Connor>OK, well, do you want to do January?
<v Connor>好的，那你想要一月吗？

00:41:51.720 --> 00:41:56.520
<v Sayre Quevedo>I mean, do you want to do it? I guess, like, that's the thing I want to figure out.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我的意思是，你想做吗？我想，这就是我想要弄清楚的事情。

00:41:56.520 --> 00:41:58.520
<v Connor>Do I want to?
<v Connor>我想做吗？

00:41:58.520 --> 00:42:10.920
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah, I mean, do you want to do it? I guess do we have to see him? I don't know. What are we going to do, like, go up to him and-- like, what are we going to get out of that, you know?
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的，我的意思是，你想做吗？我想我们非得见他不可吗？我不知道。我们要去干什么，像是，去找他，然后——我们能从那得到什么，你知道吗？

00:42:10.920 --> 00:42:45.930
<v Connor>I don't know. I mean, I just want to talk to him. I want to meet him. I want to know. I want to know his health problems. I want to know his family's shit. You know, I don't know, just basic things like that, see what he's like, if he's a dick, if he's cool, if he likes to party. I don't know. You don't know. Definitely a story. What do you want out of it?
<v Connor>我不知道。我的意思是，我只是想和他谈谈。我想见他。我想知道。我想知道他的健康问题。我想知道他家里那些破事。你知道，我不知道，只是基本的事情，看看他是什么样，他是不是个混蛋，他酷不酷，他爱不爱玩。我不知道。你不知道。绝对是个故事。你从中想得到什么？

00:42:45.930 --> 00:42:57.770
<v Sayre Quevedo>Connor wants to meet your father on the most physical plane-- cash, genetics, ancestry. You're not sure what you want. It's hard to say without knowing what you can ask for.
<v Sayre Quevedo>康纳想在最物理的层面上见到你父亲——金钱、基因、血缘。你不确定你想要什么。很难说，不知道你能要求什么。

00:42:57.770 --> 00:43:00.010
<v Sayre Quevedo>I don't know if I want anything out of it.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我不知道我想从中得到什么。

00:43:00.010 --> 00:43:09.230
<v Connor>Yeah, I think that's OK, too. But, you know, I guess if you don't want to do it, I'm not going to do it either. I'll only do it if you want to do it.
<v Connor>是的，我觉得那也可以。但是，你知道，我想如果你不想做，我也不做了。只有你想要做，我才做。

00:43:09.230 --> 00:43:14.370
<v Sayre Quevedo>No, I'm-- no, I'll do it. I want to do it because you want to do it.
<v Sayre Quevedo>不，我是——不，我会做。我想做是因为你想要做。

00:43:14.370 --> 00:43:17.290
<v Connor>So I guess we'll both do it.
<v Connor>所以我想我们俩都会做。

00:43:17.290 --> 00:43:35.410
<v Sayre Quevedo>One day, you find yourself standing across the street from your birth father's shop. You don't intend to show up. It's as if you've come out of a trance. You look up and you're there. The fall's high sun beats down on your shoulders as you stare through the window.
<v Sayre Quevedo>一天，你发现自己站在你生父理发店的对面。你并不打算出现。就好像你从恍惚中走出来。你抬头看，你就站在那儿。秋天高耸的太阳照射在你的肩膀上，你盯着窗户看。

00:43:35.410 --> 00:43:38.290
<v Mom>Did you see him?
<v Mom>你看到他了吗？

00:43:38.290 --> 00:43:41.930
<v Sayre Quevedo>I smoked a cigarette and watched him work.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我抽了一根烟，看着他工作。

00:43:41.930 --> 00:43:44.610
<v Mom>What was that like for you?
<v Mom>那对你来说是什么感觉？

00:43:44.610 --> 00:43:56.890
<v Sayre Quevedo>You watch what you believe is your father's silhouette, his hands working over the client's head. Four months since you emailed, four months since you called, and all you have to show for it is this impotent act.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你看着你认为是父亲的剪影，他的手在客人的头上工作。距离你发邮件已经过去四个月，距离你打电话已经过去四个月，你所展示的只有这种无能为力的行为。

00:43:56.890 --> 00:44:36.930
<v Sayre Quevedo>And I felt sort of like you can't do [BLEEP] to me, you know? And, like, you're not-- you can't affect me. You can't stop me from being here, you know? And I guess maybe a little hurt, too, like the feeling of oh, wow, you're just, like, living-- continuing to live your life like you always have. Like I did think about the fact that we sent him an email, and gave him a call, and left our number, and that he's, like-- still gets to live his life like he's-- like nothing is different, you know?
<v Sayre Quevedo>我感觉就像[消音]对我做不了什么，你知道吗？而且，你不是——你不能影响我。你不能阻止我在这里，你知道吗？我想也许也有一点受伤，就像那种感觉，噢，哇，你只是，就像，活着——继续过你的生活，就像你一直以来那样。因为我确实想过这样一个事实，我们发了邮件，打了电话，留下了我们的号码，而他，就像——依然能过着他的生活，就像他——什么都没改变，你知道吗？

00:44:36.930 --> 00:44:54.460
<v Ira Glass>The reason language so often fails us, despite eons of development. It's not that we fail to choose the right words or that others stopped short of hearing and then comprehending us, but that another, more honest meaning is forged between and apart from what we say in silence.
<v Ira Glass>语言之所以经常让我们失望，尽管有了万年的发展。并不是我们没能选择正确的词语，或者别人没能听懂并理解我们，而是在沉默中，在我们的言语之间和之外，锻造出了另一种更诚实的意义。

00:44:54.460 --> 00:45:15.900
<v Ira Glass>Silence, unlike language, cannot be improved upon. It has its own clarifying syntax. It reveals what we are too afraid to put into words. It is in these ellipses that our full intention and meaning are revealed. And perhaps it is our unwillingness to accept what silence declares where our suffering truly begins.
<v Ira Glass>沉默，与语言不同，是无法被改善的。它有自己清晰的语法。它揭示了我们太害怕用语言表达的东西。正是这些省略号揭示了我们全部的意图和意义。也许我们痛苦的真正开始，就在于我们不愿意接受沉默所宣告的事实。

00:45:15.900 --> 00:45:22.940
<v Mom>So you want to confront him. What is it that you want to-- what do you want to achieve with the confrontation?
<v Mom>所以你想面对他。你想要什么——你面对他想要达到什么目的？

00:45:22.940 --> 00:45:42.590
<v Sayre Quevedo>I just want him to know that we exist and that, yeah-- and that it hurts that he didn't acknowledge us, but also that we'll be fine without him.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我只是想让他知道我们存在，而且，是的——他没有承认我们确实很受伤，但也想让他知道没有他我们也会过得很好。

00:45:42.590 --> 00:45:50.270
<v Mom>By saying those things, you feel like what? Like what will happen?
<v Mom>通过说这些，你觉得是什么感觉？像是，会发生什么？

00:45:50.270 --> 00:46:10.910
<v Sayre Quevedo>I don't know. I guess in a weird way I feel like I will be able to get over my issues. I feel like yeah-- I feel it's like I can finally move past that one thing, you know?
<v Sayre Quevedo>我不知道。我猜以一种奇怪的方式，我觉得我将能克服我的问题。我觉得是的——我觉得就像我终于能越过那件事了，你知道吗？

00:46:10.910 --> 00:46:56.670
<v Mom>So do you think that's actually true? I mean, that makes perfect sense to me, sweetie, you know, that you would think that that would happen. You confront the thing that haunts you. You tell it. You end it. But the question is, does it really end it? That's the question. Does it really end it?
<v Mom>所以你认为那是真的吗？我的意思是，那对我来说完全说得通，亲爱的，你知道，你会认为那会发生。你面对缠绕你的事物。你告诉它。你终结它。但问题是，它真的终结了吗？那是问题所在。它真的终结了吗？

00:46:56.670 --> 00:47:11.070
<v Sayre Quevedo>You write your message on a blank sheet of printer paper torn in half. Of all of your attempted correspondences, this one is the most to the point-- name and intention. Why bother with anything else?
<v Sayre Quevedo>你在一张撕成两半的空白打印纸上写下你的信息。在你所有尝试的通信中，这封是最切中要害的——姓名和意图。为什么要折腾别的？

00:47:11.070 --> 00:47:32.070
<v Sayre Quevedo>Hi, you write. We're your sons. We've tried reaching out to you. We'd like to meet you. We've booked your 5 o'clock appointment. We'll be waiting across the street in front of the café and would love to speak. Connor and Sayre.
<v Sayre Quevedo>嗨，你写道。我们是你的儿子。我们尝试过联系你。我们想见你。我们预约了你5点钟的档期。我们会在马路对面咖啡馆前等着，很想谈谈。康纳和塞尔。

00:47:32.070 --> 00:47:34.310
<v Sayre Quevedo>I'm going to write his name on the front.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我要在前面写上他的名字。

00:47:34.310 --> 00:47:40.720
<v Connor>Yeah. I mean, the thing is, too, is what if he's right there?
<v Connor>是的。我的意思是，问题也是，如果他就在那儿呢？

00:47:40.720 --> 00:47:45.360
<v Sayre Quevedo>I mean, we're going to hand it to him. Yeah, So he is going to be right there.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我的意思是，我们会亲手交给他。是的，所以他会就在那儿。

00:47:45.360 --> 00:47:47.640
<v Connor>How are you going to do that?
<v Connor>你要怎么做？

00:47:47.640 --> 00:47:58.880
<v Sayre Quevedo>You have booked your birth father's last appointment of the day at his barber shop. When he calls you up for your haircut, you'll hand him the note, telling him to meet you across the street.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你预约了你生父当天最后的时间。当他叫你进去理发时，你会把字条交给他，让他到马路对面找你。

00:47:58.880 --> 00:48:00.340
<v Connor>How are you going to do that?
<v Connor>你要怎么做？

00:48:00.340 --> 00:48:08.800
<v Sayre Quevedo>Walk up to him and go, like, hi, we're you're 5 o'clock. I'm your 5 o'clock. One of us will have to do it. I'm your 5 o'clock. Hand it to him, and then we'll walk out.
<v Sayre Quevedo>走上前去说，嘿，我们是你5点钟的预约。我是你的5点钟。我们其中一个必须得去说。我是你的5点钟。递给他，然后我们走出来。

00:48:08.800 --> 00:48:10.120
<v Connor>Do you want to do that?
<v Connor>你想那样做吗？

00:48:10.120 --> 00:48:11.820
<v Sayre Quevedo>I can do it. Do you want to do it?
<v Sayre Quevedo>我可以做。你想做吗？

00:48:11.820 --> 00:48:12.360
<v Connor>No.
<v Connor>不。

00:48:12.360 --> 00:48:12.780
<v Sayre Quevedo>OK.
<v Sayre Quevedo>好的。

00:48:12.780 --> 00:48:15.880
<v Connor>Not at all.
<v Connor>一点也不。

00:48:15.880 --> 00:48:39.880
<v Sayre Quevedo>It's spring of 2022 in Los Angeles. You are 29 years old. It's been nearly a year since you first sent your email to your birth father asking to meet, nearly two years to the day since Connor first called you and asked to look for your birth father. Now your brother is here in front of you, hunched in front of the mirror, pulling his hair into two loose braids.
<v Sayre Quevedo>2022年春天，洛杉矶。你29岁。距离你第一次给生父发邮件要求见面已经过去将近一年，距离康纳第一次打电话给你要求寻找生父已经过去将近两年。现在你的兄弟就在你面前，蜷缩在镜子前，把头发扎成两根松散的辫子。

00:48:39.880 --> 00:48:43.400
<v Sayre Quevedo>It's 4:23 PM.
<v Sayre Quevedo>下午4:23。

00:48:43.400 --> 00:48:44.380
<v Connor>We should probably get going.
<v Connor>我们可能该出发了。

00:48:44.380 --> 00:48:52.280
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah, we should get going because we're going to need time to park and stuff, too. Are you ready, Connor?
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的，我们该出发了，因为我们需要时间停车之类的。你准备好了吗，康纳？

00:48:52.280 --> 00:49:06.000
<v Connor>Yeah. Let me just make sure I have everything.
<v Connor>是的。让我确认我带了所有东西。

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:15.000
<v Sayre Quevedo>You're both quiet as you walk out the door.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你们俩走出门口时都很安静。

00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:25.700
<v Sayre Quevedo>OK. OK.
<v Sayre Quevedo>好的。好的。

00:49:25.700 --> 00:49:27.000
<v Connor>How are you feeling?
<v Connor>感觉怎么样？

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:29.710
<v Sayre Quevedo>I'm scared. I'm nervous.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我很害怕。我很紧张。

00:49:29.710 --> 00:49:33.570
<v Connor>Yeah. What are you nervous about?
<v Connor>是的。你因为什么紧张？

00:49:33.570 --> 00:49:36.210
<v Sayre Quevedo>That he's going to be mean.
<v Sayre Quevedo>他会很刻薄。

00:49:36.210 --> 00:49:38.690
<v Connor>Yeah. Well, he doesn't really have the right to be mean.
<v Connor>是的。好吧，他其实没权利刻薄。

00:49:38.690 --> 00:49:41.810
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah, but that doesn't stop anyone from being mean.
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的，但这并不能阻止任何人刻薄。

00:49:41.810 --> 00:50:00.210
<v Connor>No, but I don't know. I mean, I have-- I mean, I feel defensive of you. So if he was mean to us, I'd think about it as he's mean to you, where I'd care more, I guess.
<v Connor>不，但我不知道。我的意思是，我有——我的意思是，我觉得为你感到防御。所以如果他对我们刻薄，我会认为这是他对你刻薄，那样我会更在乎，我想。

00:50:00.210 --> 00:50:07.490
<v Sayre Quevedo>That's mostly what-- I'm afraid of him being mean to you, too. I don't really care what he says to me. I don't want him to hurt you.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我大多是这样——我怕他对你也刻薄。我不在乎他对我说什么。我不希望他伤害你。

00:50:07.490 --> 00:50:10.850
<v Connor>Same here.
<v Connor>我也是。

00:50:10.850 --> 00:50:32.130
<v Sayre Quevedo>You pull down the driveway and onto the mile that separates you from your birth father. This street is ordinary to you now. In the nearly two years you have lived here, you have driven it hundreds of times, walked it drunk, sweaty, in the rain. You've kissed strangers on the street at midnight, mouth full of ash.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你开车下车道，来到那条隔开你和生父的一英里路上。这条街对你来说现在很平常。在你住在这里的近两年里，你开车经过了数百次，醉醺醺地走过，汗流浃背，雨中。你在午夜街头亲吻过陌生人，满嘴烟灰。

00:50:32.130 --> 00:50:59.290
<v Sayre Quevedo>Time widens the aperture. Certain features pass flat against the windshield as you drive. Others stay inexplicably in the fore-- the laundromat that you went to the day you arrived here, the bar with the barnhouse facade, which you will never step inside of the entire time you live in Los Angeles, the white house with busts lined across the roof and a headless angel in the yard, the psychic's front porch.
<v Sayre Quevedo>时间拓宽了光圈。某些特征在你开车经过时显得平淡。其他特征却莫名其妙地留在前方——你搬到这里那天去过的自助洗衣店，有谷仓外立面的酒吧，你在住在洛杉矶期间从未踏入过一次，屋顶排列着半身像、院子里有一个无头天使的白房子，算命先生的前廊。

00:50:59.290 --> 00:51:00.570
<v Sayre Quevedo>What time is it?
<v Sayre Quevedo>几点了？

00:51:00.570 --> 00:51:04.250
<v Connor>It's 4:34. 4:34.
<v Connor>4:34。4:34。

00:51:04.250 --> 00:51:09.640
<v Sayre Quevedo>OK.
<v Sayre Quevedo>好的。

00:51:09.640 --> 00:51:23.130
<v Connor>Ah. OK, well, we're outside of it. Hmm. This somehow got so much harder now that we're here.
<v Connor>啊。好吧，我们就在外面。嗯。既然到了这里，事情变得更加困难了。

00:51:23.130 --> 00:51:28.500
<v Sayre Quevedo>You are now 20 minutes early, which gives Connor time to reconsider the plan entirely.
<v Sayre Quevedo>你现在提前了20分钟，这给了康纳足够的时间重新考虑整个计划。

00:51:28.500 --> 00:51:35.140
<v Connor>Do you think we should try-- I know you plan on doing the note, but do you think that we should call him?
<v Connor>你认为我们应该试着——我知道你打算用字条，但你认为我们应该给他打电话吗？

00:51:35.140 --> 00:51:36.860
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah, but we already tried calling.
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的，但我们已经试过打电话了。

00:51:36.860 --> 00:51:48.500
<v Connor>But we call his barbershop and be like, hey, I've got an appointment for him at 5 o'clock. He'll say, yeah, sure, let me check.
<v Connor>但我们可以给理发店打电话，说，嘿，我给他预约了5点钟。他会说，好的，当然，让我检查一下。

00:51:48.500 --> 00:51:52.780
<v Sayre Quevedo>And then what, say exactly what's in the note? Say we're outside?
<v Sayre Quevedo>然后呢，说和字条上一样的话？说我们在外面？

00:51:52.780 --> 00:51:57.380
<v Connor>Hey, yeah, this is your sons.
<v Connor>嘿，是的，这是你的儿子。

00:51:57.380 --> 00:51:59.540
<v Sayre Quevedo>What if he hangs up?
<v Sayre Quevedo>如果他挂断了怎么办？

00:51:59.540 --> 00:52:01.140
<v Connor>Then we go in there.
<v Connor>那我们就进去。

00:52:01.140 --> 00:52:02.220
<v Sayre Quevedo>And do what?
<v Sayre Quevedo>然后做什么？

00:52:02.220 --> 00:52:03.800
<v Connor>We say, fuck you.
<v Connor>我们说，去你的。

00:52:03.800 --> 00:52:07.580
<v Connor>[LAUGHTER]
<v Connor>[笑声]

00:52:07.580 --> 00:52:21.100
<v Connor>I don't know. I'm OK with doing that. I've always wanted to say fuck you to dad. Do you want to go in together? Would that make you feel better about it?
<v Connor>我不知道。我愿意那样做。我一直想对爸爸说“去你的”。你想一起进去吗？那会让你感觉好受点吗？

00:52:21.100 --> 00:52:24.420
<v Sayre Quevedo>I mean, if you want to, I'm down.
<v Sayre Quevedo>我的意思是，如果你想的话，我奉陪。

00:52:24.420 --> 00:53:01.900
<v Connor>I want him to look at us in the case that he decides that he doesn't want to deal with it, so that he can at least do it once if he decides to avoid conversation with us, at least going in there and him seeing our face. I can be like, hey, I'm here, and I see you.
<v Connor>我希望他看着我们，以防他决定不想应付这件事，这样至少如果他决定避免和我们对话，至少进去一次让他看到我们的脸。我可以像，嘿，我在这儿，我看着你。

00:53:01.900 --> 00:53:31.470
<v Sayre Quevedo>OK, two minutes. One minute till 5:00. OK. Are you ready?
<v Sayre Quevedo>好的，两分钟。离5点还有一分钟。好的。你准备好了吗？

00:53:31.470 --> 00:53:33.070
<v Connor>Yeah, you?
<v Connor>是的，你呢？

00:53:33.070 --> 00:53:41.020
<v Sayre Quevedo>As ready as I can be.
<v Sayre Quevedo>正如我所能准备好的那样。

00:53:41.020 --> 00:53:50.310
<v Sayre Quevedo>[CHATTER]
<v Sayre Quevedo>[嘈杂声]

00:53:50.310 --> 00:53:55.270
<v Ira Glass>The first words your birth father ever says to you-- do you have an appointment?
<v Ira Glass>你生父对你说的第一句话——你有预约吗？

00:53:55.270 --> 00:53:58.670
<v Sayre Quevedo>Yeah, 5:00.
<v Sayre Quevedo>是的，5点。

00:53:58.670 --> 00:54:20.310
<v Ira Glass>You sit down and wait your turn. You can barely get yourself to look up at him. You fix your eyes first on the wine-colored tiles, then the client's sneakers slack against the footrest, the shapeless, oversized black barber's tarp, then your father's hands as he trims stray hairs, chatting easily over the jazz music playing softly over the speakers.
<v Ira Glass>你坐下来等候。你几乎无法让自己抬头看他。你先把眼睛固定在酒红色的瓷砖上，然后是客人的运动鞋在脚垫上松弛着，那件不成形的、超大的黑色理发披风，然后是你父亲的手，当他修剪杂毛时，在音箱里轻柔播放的爵士乐声中轻松聊天。

00:54:20.310 --> 00:55:04.120
<v Ira Glass>The walls are lined with framed photographs of what you assume were once famous Hollywood actors whose faces you do not recognize and names you do not know. In the corner, a magazine rack full of vintage Playboy magazines. Your birth father brushes clean his client's forehead and nape with a blond duster brush, says goodbye cheerily. He glances over at you and Connor and then calmly walks to the back door of the shop. He disappears for five seconds, then 10, then 15. He returns with a broom and slowly, methodically sweeps the hair into a dustbin and then the trash.
<v Ira Glass>墙上挂着装裱好的照片，你以为那是曾经著名的好莱坞演员，但你认不出他们的脸，也不认识他们的名字。角落里，放着一堆老式《花花公子》杂志。你生父用一把金色的除尘刷把客人的前额和后颈扫干净，愉快地说了再见。他扫了你和康纳一眼，然后平静地走到店的后门。他消失了五秒钟，然后是10秒，15秒。他带着一把扫帚回来，缓慢地、有条不紊地把头发扫进灰尘桶，然后丢进垃圾桶。

00:55:04.120 --> 00:55:50.840
<v Ira Glass>You imagine everything leading up to this moment from his point of view. Today he woke up, probably had a coffee, kissed his wife goodbye, drove to work, attended to some regulars, made small talk about the relief of 70-degree weather or the impending invasion of Ukraine as he lined up their beards and hairlines-- took a quick lunch break, scrolled on Facebook on his phone. Ten minutes ago, he probably looked up at the clock, saw two young guys walk in, and felt a wave of relief, the last customers. As he finished sweeping the hair into a bin, he was probably hit with the feeling of relaxation that comes with knowing that the day is nearly done.
<v Ira Glass>你想象着从他的角度来看导致这一刻的所有事情。今天他醒来，可能喝了杯咖啡，亲吻妻子说再见，开车上班，接待了一些常客，关于70度天气的放松或者是乌克兰即将到来的入侵闲聊，当他修剪他们的胡须和发际线时——吃了个快午餐，在手机上刷着 Facebook。十分钟前，他可能抬头看了看时钟，看到两个年轻人走了进来，感到一阵轻松，这是最后的顾客。当他把头发扫进桶里时，他可能感受到了一种因为知道一天即将结束而带来的放松感。

00:55:50.840 --> 00:57:24.490
<v Ira Glass>Now he calls those boys up to the chair. One of them, the curly-haired one who can barely look him in the eye, pulls a note from his pocket and hands it to him. His name is written on the front, and both boys walk out wordlessly. And now, what has been up until this moment a very ordinary day, has become a monumental one in just a few seconds.
<v Ira Glass>现在他叫那两个男孩坐到椅子上。其中一个，那个卷发的，几乎不敢看他眼睛的家伙，从口袋里掏出一张字条递给他。他的名字写在正面，两个男孩一言不发地走了出去。现在，直到这一刻还是非常平常的一天，在短短几秒钟内变成了一个具有纪念意义的日子。

00:57:24.490 --> 00:57:43.910
<v Ira Glass>So what you just heard was made of excerpts from the first three episodes of Reaching Out. The podcast comes from Futuro and KCRW. We are right now leaving off the story in the middle of episode 3, and I really encourage you to go hear what is about to happen. Some big, big turns are right ahead. It's very satisfying.
<v Ira Glass>所以你刚才听到的是《接触》前三集的摘录。这档播客来自 Futuro 和 KCRW。我们现在故事留在了第三集中间，我真的很鼓励你去听接下来会发生什么。一些重大的、重大的转折就在前方。非常令人满意。

00:57:43.910 --> 00:57:58.450
<v Ira Glass>Then, episode 4 comes out later this week, and you can actually listen to all six episodes right now if you subscribe to Futuro Plus. And if you dive in that way, maybe go back to episodes 1 and 2. They contain stuff you have not heard in our excerpts today.
<v Ira Glass>然后，第四集在本周晚些时候发布，如果你订阅 Futuro Plus，你实际上现在可以收听所有六集。如果你那样潜入，也许回到第一集和第二集。它们包含了一些你今天在摘录中没听到的东西。

